1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:06,023
Right now, the AI agent in your terminal can
clone itself, hand every clone

2
00:00:06,055 --> 00:00:09,562
a different job, and merge their work
while you get coffee.

3
00:00:09,594 --> 00:00:13,423
Most developers using it every single day
have no idea this exists.

4
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:18,250
And the ones who do keep making one mistake that
makes it worse than useless.

5
00:00:18,282 --> 00:00:21,146
That mistake is the last thing
I'll show you.

6
00:00:32,178 --> 00:00:33,268
First, the problem.

7
00:00:33,305 --> 00:00:35,898
A single agent has one context window.

8
00:00:35,936 --> 00:00:40,032
Think of it as a ring of memory
around its brain.

9
00:00:40,070 --> 00:00:45,293
Every file it reads, every command it runs,
every error it hits, fills that

10
00:00:45,331 --> 00:00:45,669
ring.

11
00:00:45,707 --> 00:00:49,427
And when the ring fills up, the agent
starts forgetting.

12
00:00:49,464 --> 00:00:52,809
Your instructions from ten minutes ago
don't just fade.

13
00:00:52,847 --> 00:00:53,936
They fall out.

14
00:00:59,174 --> 00:01:04,534
So instead of one agent grinding until its
memory chokes, Claude Code does

15
00:01:04,576 --> 00:01:05,366
something stranger.

16
00:01:05,407 --> 00:01:06,197
It delegates.

17
00:01:06,238 --> 00:01:07,859
It spawns a subagent.

18
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:13,261
A second, fresh copy of itself, with its own
empty context window, and

19
00:01:13,302 --> 00:01:15,338
hands it exactly one job.

20
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:21,803
The clone goes away and does the
dirty work.

21
00:01:21,844 --> 00:01:23,432
It reads twenty files.

22
00:01:23,472 --> 00:01:25,060
It runs the tests.

23
00:01:25,101 --> 00:01:27,095
It hits the dead ends.

24
00:01:27,136 --> 00:01:29,538
And then it returns one thing.

25
00:01:29,579 --> 00:01:30,352
The conclusion.

26
00:01:30,393 --> 00:01:31,981
Not the twenty files.

27
00:01:32,021 --> 00:01:33,202
Not the noise.

28
00:01:33,243 --> 00:01:34,423
One clean report.

29
00:01:37,264 --> 00:01:40,034
Which means the parent's memory
stays clean.

30
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:42,042
And that's the real trick.

31
00:01:42,082 --> 00:01:44,049
Subagents aren't about speed first.

32
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:45,254
They're about memory.

33
00:01:45,294 --> 00:01:48,466
Every messy job happens in someone
else's head.

34
00:01:53,706 --> 00:01:56,414
And here's the part nobody tells you.

35
00:01:56,453 --> 00:01:59,161
A subagent is just a markdown file.

36
00:01:59,201 --> 00:01:59,946
A name.

37
00:01:59,986 --> 00:02:00,731
A description.

38
00:02:00,771 --> 00:02:04,264
And a list of tools it's allowed to touch.

39
00:02:04,303 --> 00:02:06,226
Tools are the agent's hands.

40
00:02:06,265 --> 00:02:07,011
Reading files.

41
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:07,796
Editing code.

42
00:02:07,835 --> 00:02:08,581
Running commands.

43
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:13,683
Take the editing hand away, and you've built
a reviewer that can look

44
00:02:13,722 --> 00:02:16,038
at everything, but never break anything.

45
00:02:21,277 --> 00:02:22,412
Now scale it.

46
00:02:22,452 --> 00:02:23,195
A researcher.

47
00:02:23,235 --> 00:02:23,979
A coder.

48
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:24,762
A reviewer.

49
00:02:24,801 --> 00:02:25,545
A tester.

50
00:02:25,584 --> 00:02:30,635
Each one a separate clone, with its own memory,
its own permissions, its

51
00:02:30,674 --> 00:02:31,418
own job.

52
00:02:31,457 --> 00:02:36,507
Kick off a refactor across your codebase, one
clone per module, and they

53
00:02:36,547 --> 00:02:38,857
all run at the same time.

54
00:02:38,896 --> 00:02:41,989
What used to take an afternoon
takes minutes.

55
00:02:44,828 --> 00:02:45,787
One honest warning.

56
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:51,077
Every clone burns its own tokens doing its own
work, so a swarm is not free.

57
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:54,053
Fan out when the parallel work
pays for itself.

58
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:56,037
Not for a one line fix.

59
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:01,400
Now.

60
00:03:01,437 --> 00:03:02,133
The mistake.

61
00:03:02,170 --> 00:03:04,700
The clones are blind to each other.

62
00:03:04,736 --> 00:03:09,833
Send two of them into the same file, and they
will happily overwrite each

63
00:03:09,869 --> 00:03:10,566
other's work.

64
00:03:10,602 --> 00:03:12,766
And they're blind to you, too.

65
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:14,965
A clone never sees your conversation.

66
00:03:15,002 --> 00:03:16,799
It knows exactly one thing.

67
00:03:16,835 --> 00:03:18,632
The prompt it was handed.

68
00:03:18,668 --> 00:03:22,298
Hand it a vague prompt, and it returns
confident garbage.

69
00:03:22,335 --> 00:03:25,964
And the parent merges that garbage
straight into your code.

70
00:03:29,201 --> 00:03:30,433
So here's the rule.

71
00:03:30,464 --> 00:03:33,275
Fan out on isolated work, with self
contained instructions.

72
00:03:33,306 --> 00:03:35,801
Keep anything shared in one pair of hands.

73
00:03:35,833 --> 00:03:40,222
That is the entire difference between developers
who ship a week of work in

74
00:03:40,254 --> 00:03:44,643
a day, and the ones who let a swarm of
clones wreck their codebase.

75
00:03:47,875 --> 00:03:48,265
Delegation.

76
00:03:48,309 --> 00:03:49,133
Fresh memory.

77
00:03:49,176 --> 00:03:50,434
Tools as hands.

78
00:03:50,478 --> 00:03:51,736
Permissions as trust.

79
00:03:51,779 --> 00:03:56,941
You now understand AI agents better than most
people building with them.

80
00:03:56,985 --> 00:04:01,279
But clones sharing one brain is only
half the story.

81
00:04:01,323 --> 00:04:06,051
Because next, your agent needs to talk to
the outside world.

82
00:04:06,095 --> 00:04:10,823
That's a protocol called MCP, and it's
stranger than it sounds.

83
00:04:10,866 --> 00:04:13,860
Subscribe to codewired, and get wired in.
